{
  "result": "There's a strange kind of immortality in how we fold laundry. My grandmother taught me to match corners of a fitted sheet, a skill I have never actually mastered, but the ritual stuck. You shake it out, you wrestle with the elastic, you eventually give up and roll it into something vaguely rectangular. And yet, every time I do it, my hands remember her hands.\n\nI like to think most of our lives are like that \u2014 imperfect copies of motions we learned from people who learned them from someone else. We don't invent much. We inherit recipes, superstitions, the way we say \"it's coming down out there\" when it starts to rain. It's not original, but it's connective tissue.\n\nLately I've been obsessed with the fact that trees share food through underground fungal networks. They are not solitary competitors reaching for the sun. They are gossiping, feeding each other's seedlings, warning each other about insects. A forest is a conspiracy of care.\n\nWe like to imagine ourselves as separate houses, fenced off and self-sufficient. We're not. We are a forest trying very hard to pretend we are not a forest. And every time you fold laundry badly, or make soup from a memory, or text a friend \"thinking of you\" for no reason at all, you're sending sugar down through the roots. \n\nThat's enough. That's the whole secret to staying alive together. The rest is just learning how to fold the sheet.",
